the urethra into the

21
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:14,010
bladder, where they could
then colonize the epithelium.

22
00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:17,710
Now, in the case of upper
UTI, the bacteria can

23
00:01:17,710 --> 00:01:20,370
then continue up to the
kidneys through the ureters,

24
00:01:20,670 --> 00:01:22,950
and this is also where
they can gain access to the

25
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:25,510
blood stream with the
potential for causing sepsis

26
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:29,430
now the reason why females
are at a higher risk

27
00:01:29,430 --> 00:01:34,450
than males is due both
to a shorter urethra and

28
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:37,930
also the closer proximity
to the **** opening so the

29
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:40,670
biggest risk factor is
really just being a female

30
00:01:40,670 --> 00:01:43,870
usually those who are
younger and sexually active

31
00:01:44,570 --> 00:01:48,270
also changing the ecology
of the ****** tract so

32
00:01:48,270 --> 00:01:51,430
for instance reduction
in lactobacillus would

33
00:01:51,430 --> 00:01:54,430
change the pH and could
allow for other enteric

34
00:01:54,430 --> 00:01:57,850
microbes to colonize, which
can happen with increasing

35
00:01:57,850 --> 00:02:00,610
age and hormonal changes
like in menopause.

36
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:03,850
It can also happen with
the use of antibiotics

37
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:07,890
or somehow otherwise
weakening the immune system.

38
00:02:09,010 --> 00:02:12,790
Previous urinary tract
infections can also act as a

39
00:02:12,790 --> 00:02:15,950
risk factor, and certainly
the use of medical devices

40
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:19,410
like a catheter would
increase the risk of a UTI.

41
00:02:23,690 --> 00:02:26,430
So, there are different
ways to categorize

42
00:02:26,430 --> 00:02:29,370
urinary tract infections,
and here's a quick

43
00:02:29,370 --> 00:02:31,930
look at some of the
basic associated terms.

44
00:02:32,670 --> 00:02:35,410
UTIs take into account
a group of infections

45
00:02:35,410 --> 00:02:37,630
rather than being
a single disease.

46
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:40,770
So, they can be
categorized based on the

47
00:02:40,770 --> 00:02:42,990
part of the urinary
tract that's infected,

48
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:46,670
like the bladder,
cystitis, or infection of

49
00:02:46,670 --> 00:02:49,110
the kidney, which would
be pyelonephritis.

50
00:02:49,910 --> 00:02:52,530
Now, in the picture,
there's also urethritis

51
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:55,470
represented as a lower
urinary tract infection

52
00:02:55,570 --> 00:02:57,990
because it's part of
the urinary tract.

53
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:01,650
However, it's different
from a standard UTI

54
00:03:01,650 --> 00:03:04,310
because it's usually caused
by sexually transmitted

55
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:07,850
infections rather than
the normal gut bacteria.

56
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:13,700
UTIs can also be
asymptomatic, so asymptomatic

57
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:16,250
bacteria urea, which
means that you have

58
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:18,530
bacteria in your urine,
but there's no symptoms.

59
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:22,350
Diagnostically, this would
be the isolation of more

60
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:25,950
than 100,000 colony-forming
units per mil of a single

61
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:30,350
bacterial species in two
successive urine cultures.

62
00:03:30,870 --> 00:03:33,110
You might see this
in older patients,

63
00:03:33,450 --> 00:03:36,750
diabetics, or those
in medical facilities.

64
00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:41,430
UTIs can be classified
also as complicated or

65
00:03:41,430 --> 00:03:45,950
uncomplicated, the latter
of which here is a UTI

66
00:03:45,950 --> 00:03:49,230
that is seen in an
otherwise healthy female.

67
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:53,330
A complicated UTI,
on the other hand,

68
00:03:53,390 --> 00:03:56,570
involves all other
groups and risk factors.

69
00:03:56,570 --> 00:03:58,970
So pregnancy,

70
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:02,630
some sort of structural
urinary tract issue,

71
00:04:02,870 --> 00:04:05,110
infections in men, those with

72
00:04:05,110 --> 00:04:06,990
weakened immune systems, etc.

73
00:04:11,970 --> 00:04:14,710
Okay, let's take a closer
look at the different

74
00:04:14,710 --> 00:04:17,890
types of infections here.
and the basic presentation

75
00:04:17,890 --> 00:04:20,090
and symptoms that you
would expect from each.

76
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:23,670
So we'll start with
urethritis, and as I

77
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:27,270
mentioned, is more associated
with STIs than UTIs.

78
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:29,570
It gets categorized by the

79
00:04:29,570 --> 00:04:31,310
type of bacterial infection,

80
00:04:31,310 --> 00:04:33,850
divided either into gonococcal

81
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:36,270
or non-gonococcal urethritis.

82
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:39,110
The first of those is
caused by Neisseria

83
00:04:39,110 --> 00:04:42,890
gonorrhea, resulting
in gonorrhea, and that

84
00:04:42,890 --> 00:04:46,550
causes painful urination
and cloudy discharge.

85
00:04:46,690 --> 00:04:51,610
And the second, non
-gonococcal, is usually

86
00:04:51,610 --> 00:04:54,930
caused by bacteria other
than Neisseria, like

87
00:04:54,930 --> 00:04:57,930
another frequent STI,
Chlamydia trachomatis.

88
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,770
Now, for a standard UTI,

89
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,970
bacteria, generally
from the gut, can

90
00:05:03,970 --> 00:05:06,450
adhere to the mucosa
of the urethra

91
00:05:06,450 --> 00:05:08,770
and the bladder and
cause inflammation.

92
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:12,270
So cystitis is
infection of the bladder

93
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:14,950
and is going to
typically present with

94
00:05:14,950 --> 00:05:18,610
painful, frequent,
low-volume urination,

95
00:05:18,890 --> 00:05:22,830
along with some lower
abdominal tenderness also.

96
00:05:23,790 --> 00:05:26,990
Pylinephritis can
occur by the ascension

97
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:29,590
of bacteria to the
kidneys, or a little

98
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:32,850
less commonly through
hematogenous spread

99
00:05:32,850 --> 00:05:35,290
of bacteria from other
sites of infection.

100
00:05:35,430 --> 00:05:39,030
So there may or may not be
symptoms of lower urinary

101
00:05:39,030 --> 00:05:42,170
tract infections, but
patients with pyelonephritis

102
00:05:42,170 --> 00:05:45,490
typically present with
fever and flank pain,

103
00:05:45,910 --> 00:05:49,010
along with the possibility
of nausea and vomiting.

104
00:05:49,590 --> 00:05:51,410
Now in males,

105
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:55,530
prostatitis can also occur
if the infection proceeds

106
00:05:55,530 --> 00:05:58,610
from the urethra into the
ducts of the prostate.

107
00:05:59,210 --> 00:06:01,570
In the acute form of
this, you would expect

108
00:06:01,570 --> 00:06:03,870
to see the rapid
onset of symptoms,

109
00:06:03,870 --> 00:06:07,550
like fever and frequent
painful urination.

110
00:06:07,890 --> 00:06:10,170
Pain can also be
associated with

111
00:06:10,170 --> 00:06:12,050
physical prostate exam as well.

112
00:06:12,330 --> 00:06:13,690
Now, in contrast,

113
00:06:13,770 --> 00:06:15,730
chronic prostatitis
presents with

114
00:06:15,730 --> 00:06:18,830
more subacute onset
of the symptoms and

115
00:06:18,830 --> 00:06:20,690
some additional
pelvic discomfort.

116
00:06:21,050 --> 00:06:25,310
And there's also a divide
here in age as well, with

117
00:06:25,310 --> 00:06:28,220
younger patients commonly
being infected with

118
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:31,390
Neisseria or Chlamydia, as
opposed to older patients

119
00:06:31,390 --> 00:06:34,130
that generally are infected
with enteric bacteria.

120
00:06:39,470 --> 00:06:42,690
The diagnostic approach
to UTIs first depends on

121
00:06:42,690 --> 00:06:45,790
the presentation of
symptoms that we just talked

122
00:06:45,790 --> 00:06:49,170
about, and can then be
followed by the evaluation of

123
00:06:49,170 --> 00:06:53,430
the urine, either by a
dipstick or by microscopy.

124
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:56,970
So microscopy would allow
you to see blood cells

125
00:06:56,970 --> 00:07:00,030
in the urine, as well
as any bacteria, like

126
00:07:00,030 --> 00:07:03,110
you can see here in the
upper microscopic image.

127
00:07:03,370 --> 00:07:06,710
It's also possible to see
white blood cell casts,

128
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:09,450
like you can see in
the lower image, which

129
00:07:09,450 --> 00:07:12,090
are small tube-shaped
clusters of white blood

130
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:15,170
cells that form in the
kidney tubules and then can

131
00:07:15,170 --> 00:07:18,190
be subsequently seen
microscopically, indicating

132
00:07:18,190 --> 00:07:20,390
that the infection
involves the kidneys rather

133
00:07:20,390 --> 00:07:22,670
than just being a simple
bladder infection.

134
00:07:23,630 --> 00:07:26,290
Now, in addition to
that, dipstick tests

135
00:07:26,290 --> 00:07:28,990
can detect the
presence of the enzyme

136
00:07:28,990 --> 00:07:32,190
leukocyte esterase,
which reflects pyuria,

137
00:07:32,190 --> 00:07:35,430
as well as the presence
of nitrite, which can

138
00:07:35,430 --> 00:07:37,890
be made by certain types
of enteric bacteria

139
00:07:37,890 --> 00:07:41,030
as they convert urinary
nitrate to nitrite.

140
00:07:41,750 --> 00:07:45,570
And a final type of lab
test would involve culturing

141
00:07:45,570 --> 00:07:49,690
a urine sample, and that can
help with the identification

142
00:07:49,690 --> 00:07:53,250
of the organism causing
the infection as well as

143
00:07:53,250 --> 00:07:55,870
providing a colony count
associated with the infection.

144
00:07:56,030 --> 00:07:58,790
So we'll take a quick
look here at some

145
00:07:58,790 --> 00:08:00,590
of the media that
could be used for this.

146
00:08:04,250 --> 00:08:07,430
Okay, the media choices
for identifying a

147
00:08:07,430 --> 00:08:09,910
uropathogen typically
leverage different

148
00:08:09,910 --> 00:08:12,190
types of selective and
differential media.

149
00:08:12,430 --> 00:08:16,150
So first, a blood agar
plate can be used and

150
00:08:16,150 --> 00:08:18,850
helps to divide species
based on the destruction

151
00:08:18,850 --> 00:08:20,810
of red blood cells
that are supplemented

152
00:08:20,810 --> 00:08:23,590
into the auger,
separating the bacterial

153
00:08:23,590 --> 00:08:27,290
species into alpha, beta,
and gamma categories.

154
00:08:27,700 --> 00:08:30,470
Now, this can be used
to distinguish a beta

155
00:08:30,470 --> 00:08:33,670
-hemolytic species like
Staph aureus from a gamma

156
00:08:33,670 --> 00:08:36,570
-hemolytic species like
Staph saprophyticus.

157
00:08:37,610 --> 00:08:41,410
A muconky auger plate
can also be used, which

158
00:08:41,410 --> 00:08:44,870
is a selective end differential
plate that contains

159
00:08:44,870 --> 00:08:47,470
bile salts and crystal
violet that inhibit

160
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:50,530
gram-positive bacterial
growth and allow gram

161
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:53,990
-negative species like
enteric bacteria to grow.

162
00:08:54,270 --> 00:08:57,330
You could then differentiate
species based on lactose

163
00:08:57,330 --> 00:09:00,410
fermentation with
fermenters seen as pink

164
00:09:00,410 --> 00:09:03,710
colonies versus the colorless
colonies made by non

165
00:09:03,710 --> 00:09:06,690
-fermenters like you can see
here in the middle image.

166
00:09:07,170 --> 00:09:10,770
Now the last medium shown
on the bottom is chocolate

167
00:09:10,770 --> 00:09:14,710
agar, which would not
necessarily be used in a

168
00:09:14,710 --> 00:09:18,570
routine UTI analysis, but
instead would be useful

169
00:09:18,570 --> 00:09:21,490
if you're screening for
sexually transmitted pathogens,

170
00:09:21,490 --> 00:09:24,890
like we mentioned
before. It provides some

171
00:09:24,890 --> 00:09:29,070
extra growth factors like
hemen and NAD that are

172
00:09:29,070 --> 00:09:31,550
released by the red blood
cells in the medium during

173
00:09:31,550 --> 00:09:34,830
the preparation process
and are required by certain

174
00:09:34,830 --> 00:09:38,150
species for growth, like
Neisseria, for instance.

175
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:41,590
In other situations, the
same agar can be used

176
00:09:41,590 --> 00:09:44,290
to culture other bacteria
like Haemophilus.

177
00:09:50,450 --> 00:09:51,610
Now, even though you can go

178
00:09:51,610 --> 00:09:53,510
through bacterial
identification,

179
00:09:53,790 --> 00:09:57,470
the causative organisms
are frequently predictable,

180
00:09:57,530 --> 00:10:01,090
especially in uncomplicated
cases in healthy females.

181
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:03,830
And so routine
culturing is generally

182
00:10:03,830 --> 00:10:06,310
not necessary for
management decisions.

183
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:09,890
But there might be a reason
to identify a particular

184
00:10:09,890 --> 00:10:13,590
microbe in complicated
cases that involve other

185
00:10:13,590 --> 00:10:16,970
risk factors, like
hospital acquisitions,

186
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:21,490
associations with other
diseases, or maybe in a situation

187
00:10:21,490 --> 00:10:24,290
where the organism is
resistant to treatment.

188
00:10:25,320 --> 00:10:27,670
So here we can see
a representation

189
00:10:27,670 --> 00:10:30,850
of the main causes
of UTIs overall.

190
00:10:31,770 --> 00:10:35,210
As usual, you certainly
don't have to memorize the

191
00:10:35,210 --> 00:10:39,050
percentages, but instead, if
you just take a step back,

192
00:10:39,570 --> 00:10:43,090
taking into account
both the complicated and

193
00:10:43,090 --> 00:10:45,830
uncomplicated cases,
what you can see is that

194
00:10:45,830 --> 00:10:48,830
there's really only a
handful of bacterial species

195
00:10:48,830 --> 00:10:51,750
that cause the overwhelming
majority of cases.

196
00:10:52,610 --> 00:10:55,970
So we'll discuss some
of the details of those

197
00:10:55,970 --> 00:10:59,230
microbes that are the
most common causes,

198
00:10:59,230 --> 00:11:02,810
starting with E. coli,
or more specifically the

199
00:11:02,810 --> 00:11:06,090
pathogenic strain of E.
coli called uropathogenic

200
00:11:06,090 --> 00:11:10,250
E. coli, designated
UPEC, which on the lower

201
00:11:10,250 --> 00:11:13,930
end in healthcare-acquired
cases causes about

202
00:11:13,930 --> 00:11:17,090
30% of infections, and
on the upper end of

203
00:11:17,090 --> 00:11:21,110
community-acquired
uncomplicated cases can account

204
00:11:21,110 --> 00:11:24,490
for up to 90% of all
urinary tract infections.

205
00:11:28,710 --> 00:11:33,350
UPEC is a natural commensal
found in the GI tract, so

206
00:11:33,350 --> 00:11:36,190
the intestine serves as the
primary reservoir for the

207
00:11:36,190 --> 00:11:40,740
bacteria now in terms of
properties it's a gram-negative

208
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:43,150
bacillus like you can see
in the stained microscopic

209
00:11:43,150 --> 00:11:46,470
image here and it can
be identified from other

210
00:11:46,470 --> 00:11:50,410
enteric bacteria biochemically
in a lab generally based

211
00:11:50,410 --> 00:11:54,010
on the strong lactose
fermentation ability that it has

212
00:11:54,210 --> 00:11:57,090
which you can assess on
selective differential

213
00:11:57,090 --> 00:11:59,970
media like a McConkie
plate like we

214
00:11:59,970 --> 00:12:03,070
talked about before,
or on an EMB plate.

215
00:12:03,410 --> 00:12:07,230
And then this can be followed
by the IMVIC series of

216
00:12:07,230 --> 00:12:11,030
tests where E. coli typically
gives an expected outcome

217
00:12:11,030 --> 00:12:14,170
in the four biochemical
tests that will then

218
00:12:14,170 --> 00:12:17,830
differentiate it from other
lactose-fermenting coliforms.

219
00:12:18,050 --> 00:12:22,790
Now, in most cases, it
will be positive for

220
00:12:22,790 --> 00:12:25,190
the indole test, where
it uses tryptophanase

221
00:12:25,190 --> 00:12:27,650
to break down
tryptophan into indole.

222
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:30,670
It'll be positive for
the methyl red test

223
00:12:30,670 --> 00:12:32,970
because it performs
mixed acid fermentation

224
00:12:32,970 --> 00:12:35,530
of glucose and drops
the pH in the medium.

225
00:12:36,010 --> 00:12:39,090
And it's also
typically negative for

226
00:12:39,090 --> 00:12:42,070
both the VP test and
the citrate test.

227
00:12:43,210 --> 00:12:48,190
E. coli also displays
motility and it uses flagella

228
00:12:48,190 --> 00:12:51,350
to ascend the urinary tract
once it locates there.

229
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:55,810
Now, even though most
strains of E. coli can cause

230
00:12:55,810 --> 00:13:00,010
UTIs, it'll generally be
the serotypes that produce

231
00:13:00,010 --> 00:13:04,250
adhesins as a virulence
factor, like the expression

232
00:13:04,250 --> 00:13:08,390
of P. Peli that adhere
to digalacticides as a

233
00:13:08,390 --> 00:13:11,910
receptor, which are the
terminal disaccharide part of

234
00:13:11,910 --> 00:13:15,890
glycolipids found in host
cell membranes, and those

235
00:13:15,890 --> 00:13:18,270
are expressed on the
surface of epithelial cells.

236
00:13:18,710 --> 00:13:23,330
The strains that
express the P-pilli are

237
00:13:23,330 --> 00:13:25,750
often those that are
going to also be able

238
00:13:25,750 --> 00:13:28,170
to cause upper urinary
tract infections.

239
00:13:29,190 --> 00:13:32,810
The bacteria can also
use type 1 fimbriae that

240
00:13:32,810 --> 00:13:37,130
mediate attachment, but
those will use manno-sugar

241
00:13:37,130 --> 00:13:40,130
residues of glycoproteins
for binding instead.

242
00:13:40,330 --> 00:13:44,310
So attachment then
is going to prevent

243
00:13:44,310 --> 00:13:47,690
elimination of the bacteria
in any voided urine.

244
00:13:48,610 --> 00:13:49,810
Now,

245
00:13:50,070 --> 00:13:52,970
UPEC strains actually
rely on a diverse and

246
00:13:52,970 --> 00:13:55,730
flexible array of
different virulence factors

247
00:13:55,730 --> 00:14:00,650
besides adhesions, like
iron uptake systems, since

248
00:14:00,650 --> 00:14:04,010
iron is pretty scarce
in the urinary tract.

249
00:14:04,010 --> 00:14:09,510
And it can also produce
toxins like alpha hemolysin.

250
00:14:09,630 --> 00:14:12,130
That's going to create
pores in host cell

251
00:14:12,130 --> 00:14:14,950
membranes, which causes
lysis and tissue damage.

252
00:14:14,950 --> 00:14:18,270
in addition to the
expression of a capsule

253
00:14:18,270 --> 00:14:20,990
that can shield it from
host immune defenses.

254
00:14:26,420 --> 00:14:28,460
Okay, we'll stick
with the enterogram

255
00:14:28,460 --> 00:14:31,240
negative bacilli here
with the next microbial

256
00:14:31,240 --> 00:14:34,400
example, which is
Klebsiella pneumoniae,

257
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:37,040
also a natural member
of the gut flora.

258
00:14:37,560 --> 00:14:40,220
Now, although it's
nowhere near as

259
00:14:40,220 --> 00:14:43,320
prevalent a cause of
UTIs as E. coli is,

260
00:14:43,740 --> 00:14:48,080
I guess no other microbe
is really, but Klebsiella,

261
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:50,940
however, is usually up
there is the second most

262
00:14:50,940 --> 00:14:54,960
common cause overall, with
its prevalence typically

263
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:57,840
increasing in hospital
and medical settings.

264
00:14:58,300 --> 00:15:02,140
Now, from the perspective
of microbial properties

265
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:04,460
and characteristics, this
would be one of those

266
00:15:04,460 --> 00:15:07,520
species that could be
differentiated from E. coli by

267
00:15:07,520 --> 00:15:09,540
using all of those tests
that we just mentioned.

268
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:13,940
So Klebsiella basically
looks the same as

269
00:15:13,940 --> 00:15:16,380
E. coli under a
microscope after a gram

270
00:15:16,380 --> 00:15:19,600
stain, and it's also
a lactose fermenter.

271
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:23,480
However, when you grow it
on a MAC plate or an EMB

272
00:15:23,480 --> 00:15:26,540
agar, the level of fermentation
is going to be less

273
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:29,780
than E. coli, and so it could
be differentiated a bit.

274
00:15:30,320 --> 00:15:32,220
Now, in addition to that,

275
00:15:32,420 --> 00:15:34,940
Klebsiella will grow
on those plates, having

276
00:15:34,940 --> 00:15:38,400
colonies with a much more
wet mucoid appearance to

277
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:41,720
them, like you could see
in the picture here. And

278
00:15:41,720 --> 00:15:44,360
this appearance is brought
about by the fact that

279
00:15:44,360 --> 00:15:47,520
Klebsiella produces a
very large and prominent

280
00:15:47,520 --> 00:15:51,720
polysaccharide capsule
in comparison to E. coli.

281
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:56,060
Now, besides appearance,
if you were to run

282
00:15:56,060 --> 00:15:58,740
the IMVIC tests here,
you would get the

283
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:01,720
opposite reactions from
E. coli. So that makes

284
00:16:01,720 --> 00:16:03,700
identifying one from
the other somewhat

285
00:16:03,700 --> 00:16:06,060
straightforward from a
biochemical standpoint.

286
00:16:06,460 --> 00:16:10,000
And it's also non-modal,
again the opposite of

287
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,280
E. coli, although it does
similarly use fimbria

288
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:17,880
for attachment and also
for biofilm formation.

289
00:16:21,800 --> 00:16:25,300
Now, although E. coli
and Klebsiella happen

290
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:28,760
to be some of the top
causes of UTI, we're not

291
00:16:28,760 --> 00:16:30,800
going to go in the order
of prevalence here.

292
00:16:31,200 --> 00:16:33,720
Instead, we'll stick
with another example that

293
00:16:33,720 --> 00:16:36,300
fits in the same category
as the previous two.

294
00:16:36,700 --> 00:16:41,520
So, Proteus is a genus of
gram-negative bacteria,

295
00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:46,020
and it includes the species
Proteus mirabilis as well

296
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:49,740
as Proteus vulgaris, both
of which are normally

297
00:16:49,740 --> 00:16:52,910
found in low numbers as
commensals in the human

298
00:16:52,910 --> 00:16:57,100
gastrointestinal tract, and
both of which can cause UTIs.

299
00:16:57,560 --> 00:17:01,420
Now, proteus is
highly modal, and it

300
00:17:01,420 --> 00:17:03,840
will swarm as it grows
on a culture plate,

301
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:05,380
like you can see in
the picture here.

302
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:09,660
So the cells can differentiate
into hyperflagellated

303
00:17:09,660 --> 00:17:12,880
forms, and that allows
them to migrate in response

304
00:17:12,880 --> 00:17:16,820
to nutrient stimuli.
So as a result, they'll

305
00:17:16,820 --> 00:17:20,320
form concentric rings of
growth as the cells move away

306
00:17:20,320 --> 00:17:22,980
from the initial inoculation
site on the plate.

307
00:17:23,540 --> 00:17:25,460
Now, biochemically,

308
00:17:25,620 --> 00:17:29,660
protease does not ferment
lactose, but it does

309
00:17:29,660 --> 00:17:34,160
produce large quantities
of the enzyme urease, which

310
00:17:34,160 --> 00:17:37,920
will split urea into
carbon dioxide and ammonia.

311
00:17:38,530 --> 00:17:42,020
Now, the result of this
raises the pH of the

312
00:17:42,020 --> 00:17:47,180
urine, and it will cause
ions like magnesium and

313
00:17:47,180 --> 00:17:50,160
calcium to precipitate
out. And that's going

314
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:53,380
to lead to the formation
of magnesium ammonium

315
00:17:53,380 --> 00:17:57,320
phosphate, making struvite
crystals, and calcium

316
00:17:57,320 --> 00:18:00,620
phosphate, which produces
apatite crystals.

317
00:18:00,620 --> 00:18:03,180
And both of those can result in

318
00:18:03,180 --> 00:18:04,720
the formation of kidney stones.

319
00:18:05,590 --> 00:18:08,820
And more than that, the
increased alkalinity of the

320
00:18:08,820 --> 00:18:12,390
urine is also somewhat
toxic to the uroepithelium.

321
00:18:17,200 --> 00:18:20,620
Our next example here,
Pseudomonas aeruginosa

322
00:18:20,620 --> 00:18:23,320
is still a gram
-negative bacillus, but

323
00:18:23,320 --> 00:18:25,900
it's not usually part
of the normal flora.

324
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:29,400
Instead, it's an
opportunistic microbe that can

325
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:32,000
cause a number of
different infections and is

326
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,540
certainly one that's associated
with nosocomial infections.

327
00:18:35,540 --> 00:18:38,940
So it's going to be much
more frequently seen

328
00:18:38,940 --> 00:18:41,940
in hospital-acquired
urinary tract infections.

329
00:18:42,120 --> 00:18:45,980
It grows really well in
moist environments and it

330
00:18:45,980 --> 00:18:50,040
can be transmitted by
contaminated medical equipment

331
00:18:50,040 --> 00:18:54,020
like a urinary catheter now
the biofilm that it makes

332
00:18:54,020 --> 00:18:57,180
is derived from its
capsular secretions which is

333
00:18:57,180 --> 00:19:01,740
basically like an external
slime layer or a secreted

334
00:19:01,740 --> 00:19:05,860
exopolysaccharide matrix
and strains of it also tend

335
00:19:05,860 --> 00:19:08,440
to display a high degree
of antibiotic resistance

336
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:13,360
now in terms of identification
it it can be differentiated

337
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:17,060
from some of the more
common culprits like E.

338
00:19:17,060 --> 00:19:21,000
coli, since it doesn't
ferment lactose and is also

339
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:25,620
oxidase positive. The presence
of cytochrome C oxidase

340
00:19:25,620 --> 00:19:28,140
enzyme activity is a
characteristic that's

341
00:19:28,140 --> 00:19:31,440
associated with the aerobic
respiratory pathway in some

342
00:19:31,440 --> 00:19:35,820
microbes, and it can be
measured relatively quickly

343
00:19:35,820 --> 00:19:39,360
with a simple rapid
biochemical test in a lab that

344
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:42,560
will help you to distinguish
enteric bacteria from

345
00:19:42,560 --> 00:19:45,660
other aerobic gram-negative
bacilli like pseudomonas.

346
00:19:46,200 --> 00:19:49,780
And when it's cultured in
a lab, it also secretes

347
00:19:49,780 --> 00:19:52,280
piocyanin, like you can
see in the image here,

348
00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:56,140
and usually is going to
have a fruity smell to it.

349
00:19:56,140 --> 00:19:59,300
So identification is
generally not too difficult.

350
00:20:03,360 --> 00:20:06,560
Okay, let's move over
to the gram-positive

351
00:20:06,560 --> 00:20:10,820
species that cause UTIs.
And we'll start here with

352
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:14,100
staph saprophyticus,
which happens to be one

353
00:20:14,100 --> 00:20:16,540
of the most frequent
causes seen in younger

354
00:20:16,540 --> 00:20:19,920
sexually active females
and is rarely going to be

355
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:22,380
responsible for infections
in other patients.

356
00:20:22,780 --> 00:20:27,280
Now, as we've seen before,
it's again part of the

357
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:31,560
normal flora and generally
it's found on the skin of the

358
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:34,940
perineum as well as
being found in the lower

359
00:20:34,940 --> 00:20:40,020
gastrointestinal and genitourinary
tracts. And it really

360
00:20:40,020 --> 00:20:43,120
only causes problems if it
moves up into the bladder.

361
00:20:43,680 --> 00:20:47,280
Now, as a staph species,
you would expect to

362
00:20:47,280 --> 00:20:50,320
see gram-positive cocci
in irregular clusters

363
00:20:50,320 --> 00:20:53,400
like we can see here,
right? So no surprises.

364
00:20:53,420 --> 00:20:56,640
But biochemically, you'd
have to differentiate

365
00:20:56,640 --> 00:20:59,620
it from the other two
common staph species,

366
00:20:59,980 --> 00:21:03,240
aureus and epidermidis,
which are all going to look

367
00:21:03,240 --> 00:21:07,160
the same under the
microscope. So the tests that

368
00:21:07,160 --> 00:21:09,980
are listed here will help
you make that distinction.

369
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:14,600
So being catalase positive
separates it from the

370
00:21:14,600 --> 00:21:17,160
strep group, if there was
any question about that.

371
00:21:17,160 --> 00:21:21,240
And then the next two
features listed, coagulase

372
00:21:21,240 --> 00:21:23,660
negative and being gamma
hemolytic on a blood auger

373
00:21:23,660 --> 00:21:27,680
plate, will differentiate
it, as well as staph epi,

374
00:21:27,680 --> 00:21:32,120
for that matter from Staph
aureus which is typically

375
00:21:32,120 --> 00:21:34,800
going to be beta hemolytic
and coagulase positive.

376
00:21:34,800 --> 00:21:37,540
Now at that point you
can then distinguish

377
00:21:37,540 --> 00:21:40,980
Staph sapro by its
resistance to novobiocin

378
00:21:40,980 --> 00:21:44,680
whereas Staph epi is
usually sensitive to it.

379
00:21:48,640 --> 00:21:51,440
All right let's stick
with the gram positive

380
00:21:51,440 --> 00:21:54,160
cocci here with the
genus Anorococcus.

381
00:21:54,460 --> 00:21:58,080
This includes the species
Fecalis and Facium,

382
00:21:58,080 --> 00:22:00,680
that are both natural
enteric bacteria and

383
00:22:00,680 --> 00:22:03,240
are more frequently seen
as hospital-acquired

384
00:22:03,240 --> 00:22:07,020
UTIs, often showing
drug resistance as well.

385
00:22:07,360 --> 00:22:10,080
Now, you might recall
that Anorococcus

386
00:22:10,080 --> 00:22:13,040
was originally
classified as strep, so

387
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:15,200
it will have some of
the same features.

388
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:19,080
The cells are typically
going to be arranged in

389
00:22:19,080 --> 00:22:22,180
pairs and short chains,
like you can see here

390
00:22:22,180 --> 00:22:25,340
in the image of a gram
-stained blood culture,

391
00:22:25,340 --> 00:22:29,380
which shows many diplococci,
which are actually

392
00:22:29,380 --> 00:22:32,500
morphologically very
similar to strep pneumoniae.

393
00:22:32,940 --> 00:22:36,580
So biochemically,
we can separate the

394
00:22:36,580 --> 00:22:38,840
enterococcus group
from staph with a

395
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:41,160
catalase test, which
would be negative.

396
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:45,460
And then a blood auger would
allow you to separate out

397
00:22:45,460 --> 00:22:49,260
the definitive beta hemolytic
strep groups, like A

398
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:52,600
and B, from those that are
alpha and gamma hemolytic,

399
00:22:52,600 --> 00:22:56,900
like group D, the viridens
group, and strep pneumoniae.

400
00:22:57,320 --> 00:23:00,540
Now, from there, you could
get to enterococcus and

401
00:23:00,540 --> 00:23:03,680
group D strep with a
positive bile escalon test,

402
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:08,360
and then use a PYR test
or growth in high salt to

403
00:23:08,360 --> 00:23:11,460
separate those, with
enterococcus being positive

404
00:23:11,460 --> 00:23:14,280
for both, which is a good
way to separate it from

405
00:23:14,280 --> 00:23:16,560
something like strep
galliiticus, for instance.

406
00:23:17,820 --> 00:23:21,260
Now, using a different
dichotomous approach, you

407
00:23:21,260 --> 00:23:25,540
could also use optogen
susceptibility to separate

408
00:23:25,540 --> 00:23:29,080
enterococcus as resistant
and strep pneumoniae as

409
00:23:29,080 --> 00:23:32,620
susceptible, so just a
different diagnostic approach.

410
00:23:36,530 --> 00:23:40,680
Okay, for our last main
example, where shifting gears,

411
00:23:40,680 --> 00:23:43,620
moving away from bacteria
and taking a look at a

412
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:47,620
fungal cause which is candida
usually candida albicans

413
00:23:47,620 --> 00:23:51,000
this is a very common
human commensal and can

414
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:54,300
act as an opportunist in
certain situations like when

415
00:23:54,300 --> 00:23:57,840
it causes oral thrush or
****** yeast infections

416
00:23:58,050 --> 00:24:01,780
urinary tract involvement
with candida species generally

417
00:24:01,780 --> 00:24:05,210
affects patients with
medical devices and hospitals

418
00:24:05,210 --> 00:24:09,500
those with recent
antibiotic use people with

419
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:13,540
diabetes and also those with
weakened immune systems.

420
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:17,200
Now, the most distinguishing
feature of candida albicans

421
00:24:17,200 --> 00:24:21,180
is its ability to grow as
hyphae, as single budding

422
00:24:21,180 --> 00:24:24,520
yeast, and as pseudohyphae,
which are basically

423
00:24:24,520 --> 00:24:27,520
elongated yeast cells that
don't separate after division.

424
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:31,200
Candida albicans will
also produce germ tubes

425
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:35,100
when cultured and incubated
at 37 degrees, and

426
00:24:35,100 --> 00:24:37,740
this provides a real
classic diagnostic marker

427
00:24:37,740 --> 00:24:40,320
to distinguish it from
other candida species.

428
00:24:44,370 --> 00:24:46,940
Okay, to make this a
more exhaustive view of

429
00:24:46,940 --> 00:24:49,700
urinary tract infections,
we'll take a look here

430
00:24:49,700 --> 00:24:53,120
at a number of rare or
atypical causes of UTIs.

431
00:24:53,400 --> 00:24:57,220
The first example is
the genus Strep, our

432
00:24:57,220 --> 00:24:59,500
gram-positive cocci
that grow in chains

433
00:24:59,500 --> 00:25:01,300
like you can see in
the top image here.

434
00:25:01,780 --> 00:25:04,840
Generally, the offending
species is pyogenes,

435
00:25:04,840 --> 00:25:07,400
representing group
A strep, that in

436
00:25:07,400 --> 00:25:10,140
very rare situations
can also cause a UTI.

437
00:25:10,680 --> 00:25:13,660
But a much more common
cause from the strep

438
00:25:13,660 --> 00:25:17,080
genus would be strep A
galactii, representing

439
00:25:17,080 --> 00:25:20,040
group B strep,
which colonizes the

440
00:25:20,040 --> 00:25:23,240
genitourinary tract of
about 25% of all females.

441
00:25:23,620 --> 00:25:27,740
So this can cause UTIs,
especially in pregnant women,

442
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:30,680
and identifying it is
important during pregnancy

443
00:25:30,680 --> 00:25:33,380
because it can also cause
meningitis in a newborn.

444
00:25:34,680 --> 00:25:38,520
Another cause could be staph
aureus, or gram-positive

445
00:25:38,520 --> 00:25:41,760
cocci that grow in clusters
like we saw before,

446
00:25:42,280 --> 00:25:46,380
and also can cause
UTIs by hematogenous

447
00:25:46,380 --> 00:25:49,640
spread, and that would
typically result in kidney

448
00:25:49,640 --> 00:25:52,260
damage characterized
by renal abscesses.

449
00:25:53,020 --> 00:25:56,300
Now, similar to that
is our next example,

450
00:25:56,460 --> 00:26:00,320
mycobacterium tuberculosis,
which obviously is much

451
00:26:00,320 --> 00:26:03,640
more associated with pathology
of the lung, but also

452
00:26:03,640 --> 00:26:08,140
has the possibility of
extra pulmonary spread after

453
00:26:08,140 --> 00:26:11,500
reactivation of latent
infection to other locations in

454
00:26:11,500 --> 00:26:15,760
the body, like the kidneys
and the urinary tract. And

455
00:26:15,760 --> 00:26:18,800
that's going to result in
genitourinary tuberculosis.

456
00:26:19,600 --> 00:26:23,060
Now, recall that
mycobacterium is technically

457
00:26:23,060 --> 00:26:26,780
gram-positive, but because
of the mycolic acid in

458
00:26:26,780 --> 00:26:29,340
their cell walls, acid
-fast staining is used to

459
00:26:29,340 --> 00:26:32,120
identify them
microscopically, like the red

460
00:26:32,120 --> 00:26:34,580
bacilli that you can see
here in the lower image.

461
00:26:36,040 --> 00:26:39,860
Okay, the Enterobacteriaceae
is a large

462
00:26:39,860 --> 00:26:42,960
family of gram-negative
rod-shaped bacteria

463
00:26:42,960 --> 00:26:45,560
that can be found in
the environment as well

464
00:26:45,560 --> 00:26:47,790
as in the gut of humans
and other animals.

465
00:26:48,240 --> 00:26:51,360
Some of the more frequently
seen members we've covered

466
00:26:51,360 --> 00:26:55,620
already, like E. coli,
Klebsiella, and Proteus.

467
00:26:55,780 --> 00:26:58,840
But other members of
the family that are

468
00:26:58,840 --> 00:27:01,900
more or less
opportunistic can also be

469
00:27:01,900 --> 00:27:04,780
associated with UTIs
in certain situations,

470
00:27:04,780 --> 00:27:08,280
like Enterobacter, Citrobacter,

471
00:27:08,540 --> 00:27:12,960
Serratia, or Providentia.
Now, these collectively

472
00:27:12,960 --> 00:27:16,300
make up a small but important
percentage of cases and

473
00:27:16,300 --> 00:27:18,940
are generally associated
with hospital infections.

474
00:27:21,300 --> 00:27:23,900
Carinibacterium
urelyticum is a less

475
00:27:23,900 --> 00:27:27,580
frequent cause of cystitis
or pyelonephritis.

476
00:27:27,680 --> 00:27:31,440
These are slow-growing
gram-positive bacilli that,

477
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:35,380
similar to proteus, make the
enzyme urease, and so it's

478
00:27:35,380 --> 00:27:37,980
also associated with the
formation of kidney stones.

479
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:43,240
schistosoma is actually
a parasitic infection not

480
00:27:43,240 --> 00:27:46,840
bacterial but it can still
affect the urinary system

481
00:27:46,840 --> 00:27:52,620
it's a a freshwater fluke a
worm classified as a trematode

